  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nAn occluded front will bring rain on Sunday. Rain may end as\na brief period of snow Sunday night followed by temperatures \ndropping below freezing which produce some icy surfaces early \nMonday morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA large trough enters the area tomorrow with a strong cold front\nout ahead of it. Rain will develop and move east over the area\nthroughout the day. Widespread 0.5 to 0.75 inches of rain is\nexpected for the area. As the front passes, temperatures will\ndrop and lead to snow as precipitation exits the area late\nSunday night. The greatest chances for snow are in the North\nWoods and St. John Valley as temperatures will drop there first.\n0.5 to 1.5 inch of snow is possible from the Central Highlands\nnorth. This precipitation could lead to rivers slowly rising but\nflooding is not expected. \n\nCold air behind the front will lead to freezing temperatures\nfrom the north to The Airline. Water on roads could quickly \nfreeze as temperatures in the north fall into the 20s overnight.\nThis could lead to some slick roads, impacting travel for the \nMonday morning commute. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nStrong snow showers possible across the North Monday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA strong full latitude trough pushes into the Eastern Seaboard\non Monday. Across Aroostook and northern Somerset, Piscatiquis \nand Penobscot counties, the associated forcing, coupled with \nlow level lapse rates of 7.5 to 8.5 C/km and a little over 100 \nJ/kg of CAPE should be sufficient to cause snow showers, some of\nwhich could be locally strong. However, there is not a lot of \nwind from around 700 mb to the surface (less than 25kt) to \nsupport snow squalls. While there could be a quick accumulation \non grassy surfaces, the mid April sun angle should limit any \naccumulation on paved surfaces. However, visibilities could \nstill be reduced to less than 1 mile, in any location that \nreceives a strong snow shower. Across Downeast Maine, there \nshould not be sufficient forcing to support snow showers. In \nbetween, while snow showers cannot be ruled out, the confidence \nin occurrence is not high enough to reflect in the forecast at \nthis time.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nCooler to start and mostly dry Tuesday-Saturday with no \nsignificant systems expected.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\nThe region is under NW flow aloft Tuesday through Saturday, as\nit remains in between a cutoff low over the Maritimes and a deep\nlayered ridge axis that slowly moves eastward from the Plains. \nA short wave embedded in the flow is progged to pass by\nWednesday and Wednesday night, bringing with it a chance for\nsome showers from late Tuesday night into Wednesday night.\nThey should start as snow showers, then transistion to all\nrainshowers during the day on Wednesday from south to north.\nSnow could mix back in before ending across the north Wednesday\nnight. At this time no significant accumulation or impact is\nexpected.\n\nTemperatures gradually moderate from around 40 to the mid 40s on\nTuesday to from around 50 to the mid 50s on Saturday.",
